/-
# The finite near core admits no cancellation inside the sampling window

`NearCoreDeterminantTaper` reduces the whole surviving off-ordinate energy of the
three-taper observer to one scalar,

    ∫ q(u) · S(t, J; u) du ,
    S(t, J; u) = ∑_{σ ∈ nearOffFinset t J} m_σ · cosh(a_σ u) · cos((b_σ − t) u) ,

`q` the determinant taper, and leaves as the analytic obligation an *estimate* of that
target-centred finite exponential sum: a bound `B_near` small enough that, together with
the far remainder and the gamma bracket, it undercuts the cluster margin.

This module settles what that finite sum actually does in the regime the lane uses.
The high-ordinate taper is supported in `|u| ≤ Λ` with `Λ` of order `|t|⁻¹`, while the
cutoff `J` is a fixed accuracy parameter, so `J · Λ ≤ π/2`: *every* phase
`(b_σ − t) u` occurring in the sum lies in `[−π/2, π/2]`.  Consequently

* every cosine factor is nonnegative, indeed `≥ cos(J Λ)`;
* every `cosh` factor is `≥ 1` and `≤ cosh(Λ/2)` (heights lie in the critical strip).

So for a nonnegative determinant taper the finite near core is **pinned between two
positive multiples of the near mass**:

    nearMass(t,J) · cos(JΛ) · ∫ q  ≤  ∫ q · S  ≤  nearMass(t,J) · cosh(Λ/2) · ∫ q ,

    nearMass(t,J) = ∑_{σ ∈ nearOffFinset t J} m_σ .

There is no cancellation to find: inside the sampling window the near zeros all pull in
the same direction, and the finite core is of the size of the *number of zeros within
distance `J` of the target ordinate*, not of the cluster margin.

The consequences recorded here are honest no-gos, not estimates:

* `not_scalar_bound_of_lower_bound`, `not_scalar_bound_of_narrow_window` — whenever the
  pinned lower bound already reaches the consumer threshold, the scalar hypothesis of
  `normSqP_elim2_offVec_lt_of_scalar_bound` is unsatisfiable, so the direct
  phase-sensitive route cannot close the lane at that cutoff and that taper;
* `not_normSqP_elim2_offVec_lt_of_narrow_window` — the same statement transported to the
  surviving energy itself.

Nothing here asserts or assumes the Riemann Hypothesis, and nothing here weakens
`RiemannHypothesisFor`.  No `sorry`, no `axiom`, no Boolean receipt, no numerical
evidence.
-/
import Zeta23Bridge.NearCoreDeterminantTaper

noncomputable section

open scoped Real
open Zeta23 MeasureTheory

namespace Zeta23Bridge
namespace NearCoreNarrowWindowNoCancellation

open Zeta23Bridge.LiteralWeilParityBalance
open Zeta23Bridge.LiteralWeilSameOrdinateEvenCone
open Zeta23Bridge.LiteralWeilOtherZerosChannel
